  "facts": [
    "The Talbot Memorial Bridge, finished in 1978, stretches 22 metres across the Liffey, a wide span that once surprised commuters with its sleek, modern design by De Leuw, Chadwick and O’hEocha.",
    "The bridge marks the furthest point up the Liffey that tall ships may reach, because every bridge downstream is a swing or bascule, making the Talbot Memorial Bridge a critical maritime marker.",
    "Named after temperance champion Matt Talbot, the bridge carries a statue at its south end and sits beside Memorial Road, which honors Dublin Brigade members who fell during the 1919 Custom House raid."
  ],
